DATA SERIALIZATION COMPLETE NOTES

---

1. WHAT IS SERIALIZATION

Definition:
Serialization = Object ko convert karna into byte stream / string format so that it can be stored or transmitted

Deserialization = Byte stream se wapas object banana

Core Problem:
In-memory objects directly network ya disk pe use nahi ho sakte

Reasons:
Different machines → different memory layout
Different languages → incompatible formats
Network → sirf bytes transfer hota hai

Flow:
Object → Serialize → Network/File → Deserialize → Object

Real Life Analogy:
Food cooking → packing → delivery → unpacking

---

2. WHY SERIALIZATION IS NEEDED

Data transfer between services
Data persistence (file/database)
Inter-language communication
Microservices communication

Without serialization:
Systems ek dusre ko samajh nahi paayenge

---

3. JSON (BASIC SERIALIZATION)

JSON = JavaScript Object Notation

Example:
{
"id": 101,
"name": "Anubhav"
}

Advantages:
Human readable
Easy debugging
Language independent
Widely supported

Disadvantages:
Large size due to field names
Slow parsing
No strict schema
Weak type safety

Use cases:
APIs (REST)
Frontend-backend communication

Problem:
Large scale systems me inefficient

---

4. NEED FOR EFFICIENT SERIALIZATION

Problem with JSON:
Repeated field names
More bandwidth usage
Higher latency
Slower parsing

Goal:
Reduce size
Increase speed
Ensure compatibility

---

5. PROTOCOL BUFFERS (PROTOBUF)

Concept:
Binary serialization format by Google

Schema Definition:
message User {
int32 id = 1;
string name = 2;
}

Working:
Schema define → compiler generate code → binary encoding

Key Idea:
Field names remove karke field numbers use karte hain

Example:
JSON → "name": "Anubhav"
Protobuf → 2: "Anubhav"

Advantages:
Compact size
Fast serialization/deserialization
Strong typing
Backward compatible

Disadvantages:
Not human readable
Schema maintain karna padta hai
Debugging difficult

Use cases:
High performance systems
Internal service communication
gRPC

---

6. APACHE THRIFT

Concept:
Serialization + RPC framework

Schema Example:
struct User {
1: i32 id
2: string name
}

Features:
Cross language support
Service definition support
RPC built-in

Advantages:
Supports remote procedure calls
Flexible

Disadvantages:
More complex than protobuf
Learning curve high

Use cases:
Microservices communication

---

7. PROTOBUF VS THRIFT

Protobuf:
Only serialization
Simple
Fast

Thrift:
Serialization + RPC
More flexible
More complex

---

8. SCHEMA EVOLUTION

Definition:
Time ke saath schema change hone ki ability without breaking system

Problem:
Old and new systems must coexist

Example:

Old:
User {
int id = 1;
}

New:
User {
int id = 1;
string email = 2;
}

Scenarios:
Old service reads new data
New service reads old data

Solution:
Unknown fields ignore ho jaate hain

Rules:
Field numbers change nahi karna
Fields remove nahi karna directly
New fields optional hone chahiye
Deprecated fields mark karo

Importance:
Backward compatibility
Forward compatibility

---

9. APACHE AVRO

Concept:
Schema-based serialization system designed for big data

Key Idea:
Schema and data tightly coupled hote hain
Dynamic schema handling

Advantages:
Schema evolution friendly
No need for code generation
Compact

Disadvantages:
Less strict typing than protobuf
Runtime schema handling

Use cases:
Big data pipelines
Kafka
Hadoop ecosystem

---

10. PROTOBUF VS AVRO

Protobuf:
Static schema
Code generation required
Better for RPC

Avro:
Dynamic schema
Better for data pipelines
Schema evolution easier

---

11. SCHEMA REGISTRY

Definition:
Central place jahan schemas store hote hain

Purpose:
Maintain schema versions
Ensure compatibility

Working:
Producer schema register karta hai
Schema ID milta hai
Data ke saath ID send hota hai
Consumer ID se schema fetch karta hai

Benefits:
Loose coupling
Version control
Backward compatibility

Use cases:
Kafka systems
Event-driven architectures

---

12. COMPLETE DATA FLOW

Client creates object
Object serialized
Data transmitted
Receiver deserializes
Object reconstructed

